today we're going to be talking about  what you need to know about law school  exams so you can crush your next final  so stick around and get an edge in law  school so where to begin about law  school exams well the first thing you  should know is that almost all law  school exams consist of issues spotting  essays sometimes called hypotheticals or  hypose for short sometimes you'll see  multiple-choice or term papers in  classes but in your first year of law  school they are almost always issue  spotters the first thing you'll notice  about an issue spotting exam is that it  starts with a big story a big story  where bad things happen if it were a  criminal law exam your average issue  spotting exam would consist of people  running around murdering conspiring  assaulting and worse if it was a  Property exam you'd probably have some  sort of land dispute between different  parties or restrictions on land it was a  contract exam you'd have a contract  where some people would claim it with a  contract and some people would claim as  an invalid contract there will be lots  of people doing lots of things to each  other  that came out wrong then anyway at the  end of the story there will be a prompt  the prompt usually consists of something  very vague like explaining the rights of  the various parties or what claims does  mrs. Smith have against mr. Jones  the reason is called an issue spotting  exam is that you're going to have to  cull through those facts to come up with  the issues that need to be discussed and  here is the main thing that you need to  understand an issue spotter is not about  regurgitating the law or just  regurgitating the facts in a college  essay exam you get lots of points simply  for writing down things that you  memorized so if you are taking a class  on American history and the essay asked  about the American Revolution you'd  probably get lots of points for talking  about when the important battles were  fought the causes of the Revolution the  name of the British taxes the names the  founding father is a net cetera et  cetera facts and figures don't get you  any points in law school generally  you're an aligned  the facts as a litigator would and it's  all about application you're looking for  claims and counterclaims and defenses  and counter defenses that one party can  bring against the other or that they can  use to protect against the claims of  another party an issue spotting exam is  all about the application and you have  to learn the law and then apply the  facts they're given to you in the issue  spotter to the law that you know in this  way it's kind of like a mechanical  engineering exam you can imagine that if  an engineering professor spent a  semester explaining how a car works it  would be fair game at the end to ask the  students to design a car for the final  exam it applies what they have learned  and law school exams are sort of the  same way you are applying the law to a  given set of facts memorizing the law is  of course necessary but those are the  table stakes in law school knowing the  law will get you through the door but  knowing the law alone won't even get you  a C let alone a B or an a on the test  learning how to apply the law is a skill  it takes practice but it's a skill that  can be learned what I hate is that so  many professors claim that essay writing  is an art that is incapable of being  learned in fact although they would not  admit it to you the professor has an  interest in making you think that is  some dark art that can't be learned your  professor wants you to believe that some  people have it and some people don't but  this is just nonsense no one is born  with the skills of being able to write a  law school exam that's crazy it's just a  skill and just like learning to ride a  bike or making a free throw or learning  guitar you can learn the skills that are  important for a law school essay exam  and believe me if you can learn to pass  the else acts then you can learn to  answer an issue spotting exam during  your first year they almost all tend to  be issue spotting exams but the good  news is that you can learn how  to beat those tests it's a skill it just  takes practice and overtime you'll get  better at them so don't worry if you  don't start out writing a worthy issue  spotting exam essays no one starts out  that way it just takes practice and with  practice you'll get better and if you  take practice exams you can get an edge  on everyone else in your class oh and by  the way if you want to get better grades  on your next law school final check this  out we put together a free ebook called  the ultimate guide to crushing law  school finals just click the link below  to download it'll show you exactly how  to prepare for your next final including  how to plan how to outline and how to  study for your next fine just click  below also if you liked this video  please subscribe to our Channel we come  out with new videos on how to crush law  school all the time so thanks from  everyone here at legal eagle  [Music]
 

